from ./Package import Package
from ./Dependencies import Dependencies
from ./ImportingAndUsing import ImportingAndUsing

from ../tooling/cli/package-management import
    Remove,
    Tether,
    Untether,
    Update

export anchor PackageManagement:
    description:
        Piton supports package management and dependencies.

        Package sources are git repos and can be pinned to a specific commit,
        branch, or tag.  If no specifier is provided, the latest commit on the
        primary/default branch is used.

        The strategy for packages is managed vendored dependencies.  What that
        means is installed packages are stored as part of the project, committed
        to version control, but can be added, updated, removed, and inspected by
        the package management tooling.

    packages: {Package}
    dependencies: {Dependencies}
    importingAndUsing: {ImportingAndUsing}

    commands:
        - @{Remove}
        - @{Tether}
        - @{Untether}
        - @{Update}

    locations:
        Tethered packages are stored in the same directory as the
        piton.config.pi file inside a tethers/ directory.

        When a package is untethered, they've moved into specRoot/untethered and
        the LSP should be used to rework any imports.

    clone:
        Cloning with git (tethering) should remove any traces of git; these are
        just plain files now once they're tethered.

    conflictResolution:
        When a package is updated (or tethered or anything else), it should
        check the current status of the files against the locked version, and
        only if there are no differences can it update.  If it appears that the
        package has been modified, the user should be informed of the problem
        and asked to untether.
